uence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "callout": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "formula": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "table": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "code": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "compare": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "grid": "create-content: Author static prose and layout blocks for a LectureDoc lecture page — hero, statement, list, agenda, callout, formula, table, code, compare, and grid. Use when a lecture needs narrative or nominal lookup rather than a structural diagram, interactive simulation, quiz, or code runtime. Affordances: prose, fixed-comparison, fixed-sequence, exact-values, read-only-code. Learner actions: read, recall, inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: visible-claim, fixed-steps, exact-lookup, code-example. Limitations: no learner-controlled state, no code execution.", "flow": "create-diagram: Author structural diagram, graph, flow, and timeline blocks for a LectureDoc lecture. Use graph for explicit named edges, branching, hierarchy, or convergence; diagram when a fixed shape carries the relationship; flow for one simple linear chain; timeline only for events with explicit times or stages. Use state-sim when understanding depends on intermediate states or transformations rather than a fixed relationship. Affordances: spatial-structure, node-edge-relations, hierarchy, sequence, chronology. Learner actions: inspect, trace, compare. Evidence outputs: named-relations, fixed-structure, linear-sequence, chronology. Limitations: fixed-state-only, no-coordinate-proof, no-hidden-transition.", "timeline": "create-diagram: Author structural diagram, graph, flow, and timeline blocks for a LectureDoc lecture. Use graph for explicit named edges, branching, hierarchy, or convergence; diagram when a fixed shape carries the relationship; flow for one simple linear chain; timeline only for events with explicit times or stages. Use state-sim when understanding depends on intermediate states or transformations rather than a fixed relationship. Affordances: spatial-structure, node-edge-relations, hierarchy, sequence, chronology. Learner actions: inspect, trace, compare. Evidence outputs: named-relations, fixed-structure, linear-sequence, chronology. Limitations: fixed-state-only, no-coordinate-proof, no-hidden-transition.", "diagram": "create-diagram: Author structural diagram, graph, flow, and timeline blocks for a LectureDoc lecture. Use graph for explicit named edges, branching, hierarchy, or convergence; diagram when a fixed shape carries the relationship; flow for one simple linear chain; timeline only for events with explicit times or stages. Use state-sim when understanding depends on intermediate states or transformations rather than a fixed relationship. Affordances: spatial-structure, node-edge-relations, hierarchy, sequence, chronology. Learner actions: inspect, trace, compare. Evidence outputs: named-relations, fixed-structure, linear-sequence, chronology. Limitations: fixed-state-only, no-coordinate-proof, no-hidden-transition.", "graph": "create-diagram: Author structural diagram, graph, flow, and timeline blocks for a LectureDoc lecture. Use graph for explicit named edges, branching, hierarchy, or convergence; diagram when a fixed shape carries the relationship; flow for one simple linear chain; timeline only for events with explicit times or stages. Use state-sim when understanding depends on intermediate states or transformations rather than a fixed relationship. Affordances: spatial-structure, node-edge-relations, hierarchy, sequence, chronology. Learner actions: inspect, trace, compare. Evidence outputs: named-relations, fixed-structure, linear-sequence, chronology. Limitations: fixed-state-only, no-coordinate-proof, no-hidden-transition.", "freeform": "create-freeform: Author a freeform block for a LectureDoc lecture — the long-tail fallback for a visual relationship that none of the formal block types can express, drawn as inline SVG/HTML restricted to theme colour tokens. Reach for it only after checking the structured types: graph covers trees, DAGs and branching flowcharts; chart covers data; diagram covers fixed shapes; grid and sim.widget cover custom layouts and interactive pieces. When it genuinely is the right tool it now renders as normal page content, so use it without hedging.", "geometry-sim": "create-geometry-sim: Plan a spatial constraint simulation for a LectureDoc lecture. Use when coordinate mapping, dragging, boundaries, vectors, geometric construction, or spatial invariants are themselves the learning evidence. Do not use merely because a state diagram has positioned nodes; AVL rotations remain state-sim. The final block lowers to sim.widget. Affordances: drag, coordinate-map, constraint, construction, spatial-transform. Learner actions: manipulate, construct, predict, inspect. Evidence outputs: coordinate-change, constraint-preservation, spatial-invariant. Limitations: not-decorative-layout, not-discrete-structure, not-code-execution.", "stats": "create-infographic: Author stats blocks for a LectureDoc lecture. Use only to spotlight 2-6 standalone key numbers whose individual magnitude is the evidence; use chart for trends, distributions, or correlations and create-diagram for structural relationships. Affordances: standalone-metrics, fixed-state. Learner actions: inspect, compare. Evidence outputs: key-number. Limitations: no-trends, no-structural-relations, no-unsourced-numbers.", "model-sim": "create-model-sim: Plan a parameterized model simulation for a LectureDoc lecture. Use when learners must change real parameters and observe a recomputed quantitative consequence, compare model regimes, or inspect deterministic or stochastic causal behavior. Use chart for a fixed dataset and state-sim for discrete algorithm steps. Lowers to the shared sim runtime. Affordances: parameter-control, recompute, comparison, stochastic, history. Learner actions: manipulate, predict, calculate, compare. Evidence outputs: parameter-output-map, regime-change, quantitative-cause. Limitations: not-fixed-dataset, not-code-execution, not-discrete-structure.", "quiz": "create-quiz: Author a quiz block for a LectureDoc lecture — objective (multiple-choice, auto-graded) or subjective (open discussion prompt). Reach for it whenever a page should check understanding or provoke reflection rather than only present — at least one per lecture is encouraged. Objective for a checkable fact/skill, subjective for open-ended thinking. Affordances: elicited-response, auto-grading, misconception-diagnosis. Learner actions: predict, calculate, judge, explain. Evidence outputs: student-answer, correctness-feedback, explanation. Limitations: one assessment chain per block.", "state-sim": "create-state-sim: Plan a discrete state simulation for a LectureDoc lecture. Use whenever learning evidence depends on an algorithm's intermediate states, stepwise execution, tree/graph/array mutation, structural transformation, replay, or before-to-after mapping. AVL rotations and data-structure updates belong here even though nodes have positions. The final block lowers to sim.widget. Affordances: state-transition, step, reset, playback, history, comparison. Learner actions: trace, predict, manipulate, construct. Evidence outputs: intermediate-state, transition-sequence, before-after-map. Limitations: not-static-snapshot, not-code-execution, not-coordinate-constraint."}, "currentBrief": {"objective": "学生能写出 AVL 树节点结构定义和递归插入框架", "learningAction": "read", "requiredEvidence": "能说出节点包含的字段（key, left, right, height）", "keyClaim": "节点包含高度字段，递归插入后回溯更新高度", "misconception": "平衡因子需要单独存储", "visualTask": "显示代码和递归调用栈", "evidencePolicy": "provided"}, "currentScene": {"id": "node-structure", "kind": "content", "eyebrow": "工程实现", "headline": "节点结构与递归插入框架", "lead": "AVL 树节点包含键、左右子指针和高度字段，插入采用递归方式。", "notes": "用 code 和 list 展示节点定义和插入步骤", "blocks": [{"type": "code", "language": "python", "source": "class AVLNode:
    def __init__(self, key):
        self.key = key
        self.left = None
        self.right = None
        self.height = 1  # 节点高度，平衡因子 = 左子树高度 - 右子树高度", "filename": "avl_node.py", "caption": "AVL 树节点结构定义", "id": "b1_struct"}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"lead": "空树", "text": "若当前节点为 `null`，创建新节点并返回。", "icon": "sparkles"}, {"lead": "比较", "text": "若插入键小于当前节点键，递归插入左子树；否则递归插入右子树。", "icon": "plug"}, {"lead": "更新", "text": "插入后回溯，更新当前节点高度为 `max(left.height, right.height) + 1`。", "icon": "plug"}, {"lead": "平衡", "text": "计算平衡因子（左右子树高度差），若绝对值大于 1 则进行旋转调整。", "icon": "cloud"}, {"lead": "返回", "text": "返回调整后的节点指针。", "icon": "compass"}], "id": "b2_struct"}], "layout": {"kind": "index", "steps": [{"label": "节点结构定义", "blockIds": ["b1_struct"]}, {"label": "递归插入步骤", "blockIds": ["b2_struct"]}]}}, "qualityIssues": ["缺少递归调用栈可视化，不满足 visualTask 要求，需添加调用栈图示或动画", "递归插入框架仅有文字步骤，缺少代码示例，不足以支持学生写出递归插入代码，需添加递归插入函数代码块", "更新步骤未处理空子树情况，可能引起误解；修改列表项文本，明确当子树为空时高度视为0，例如'更新当前节点高度为 max(left.height if left else 0, right.height if right else 0) + 1'", "未明确纠正'平衡因子需要单独存储'的误解；在代码注释或旁边添加说明，如'平衡因子无需单独存储，可通过左右子树高度差计算'"]}

